{
 "what": "100000 CTAP2 getAssertion responses, ns per record. memory_*: encode into one padded span. lmdb_*: a write txn with mdb_put MDB_APPEND|MDB_RESERVE through a writer, one commit. Arm old: main 47b9ec5. Arm new: head_write takes a template parameter Exact; only the writer path of cbor::encode sets it and writes straight into the span that allocate returns.",
 "why": "The first form of the exact writer put a check into every head and cost 7 to 15 percent. This form keeps the check out of every other path.",
 "how": "Google Benchmark, one binary per arm, each built twice (a, b). 5 rounds alternating old_a new_a old_b new_b, --benchmark_repetitions=5 --benchmark_enable_random_interleaving=true. LMDB: a new env per iteration, timing paused for create and delete; MDB_WRITEMAP|MDB_NOSYNC|MDB_NOMETASYNC, MDB_INTEGERKEY.",
 "finding": "The padded paths read equal (within 1 ns, noise up to 0.9 ns). The LMDB write through the writer is 12 ns faster with fixed fields (129.6 against 141.9) and 21 ns faster with variable fields (186.6 against 208.0), because no std::string is made and copied. The assembly of generic::encode into a span differs by one lea and a register choice; its speed is equal.",
